edb/ref 17592186083576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Permutation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083311 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e9c145aa-bcbf-54bd-ba88-93f10aeb00f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1045 "Permutations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083409 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "35a2312a-8238-5672-8715-5b99766aa324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083810 #edb/ref 1071 "How many different arrangements of gold, silver, and bronze medalists can there be out of $10$ Olympic athletes, assuming there are no ties?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdbad7c7-dae3-5567-865d-d9e1a1c8b99b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083810 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083810 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ athletes, so $n=10.$ We want to select $3$ of those athletes to receive gold, silver, or bronze medals, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to award the medals is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{7!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =720.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084117 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084117 #edb/ref 1061 20569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084117 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f34cb92-fb54-5dc9-8f17-9675bc62c341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084117 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084118 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084118 #edb/ref 1061 20570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084118 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f972487c-b2d6-5f63-935f-fb91b47f99b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084118 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084119 #edb/ref 1061 20571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084119 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39129bcf-9379-546f-a458-1226fc31f8fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084119 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084120 #edb/ref 1061 20573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a131104f-d99f-5253-bcb9-6403049716e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084246 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084246 #edb/ref 1061 27561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084246 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5daedd62-435e-56b3-aa28-cfa9a1b10d0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084246 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084247 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084247 #edb/ref 1061 27676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084247 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccdc7daa-24d6-512d-8379-a36e4ffbe8a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084247 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084448 #edb/ref 1064 "A **permutation** is a selection of objects in which order matters. Two selections of the same items are counted as different selections if they are in a different order.\n\nFor example, suppose that we have $5$ books, but our bookshelf can only hold $3.$ We want to place $3$ books on the shelf and the rest will stay in the drawer. \n\nHow many permutations are there? In other words, how many ways can we place $3$ books on the shelf (in a particular order)?\n\nLet's think about all the choices we have as we fill up the bookshelf:\n\n- We can place any of our $5$ books in the first slot of the bookshelf.\n\n- Now that we've placed one book on the bookshelf, we have $4$ books remaining. We can place any of these $4$ remaining books in the second slot.\n\n- Now that we've placed two books on the bookshelf, we have $3$ books remaining. We can place any of these $3$ remaining books in the third slot.\n\nThe number of choices we have for each slot is shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{5 choices} & \\text{4 choices} & \\text{3 choices}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of arrangements, we can use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n5\\times4\\times3 = 60.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084448 #edb/ref 1065 3682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084448 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0e27ded9-9ee6-527a-9381-e7f6d0396030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084448 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084471 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\n$$\n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books and $r=3$ slots in the bookshelf. Using the formula, the total number of permutations is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3\\times 2!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3\\times \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =5\\times 4\\times 3 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** We can write ${}_nP_r$ in a number of ways:\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r = {}^nP_r = P(n,r)\n$$\n\nAll of these mean the same thing." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084471 #edb/ref 1065 4477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084471 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7e83bfaa-0308-5b07-a516-02b67001ac8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084471 #edb/ref 1067 "The General Formula for Permutations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253958 #edb/ref 1080 "56\\,040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253958 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253958 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20b1c87d-2bb9-5316-8b06-ba2ad49aa8bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254698 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254698 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254698 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74455ded-e565-5af1-b02c-a8c28c379286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254739 #edb/ref 1080 "20\\,160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254739 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254739 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "790a3132-0dff-5b56-91d0-b735d0135954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255351 #edb/ref 1080 "40\\,320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255351 #edb/ref